# /etc/dnsmasq.d — DNS configuration for vmhost (10.211.55.3)

## Topology

This VM acts as the primary DNS resolver for the Mac host (via Parallels).

    Mac (mDNSResponder)
      → 10.211.55.3:53  (this VM, primary)
      → 1.1.1.1         (fallback)
      → 10.0.0.1      (CorpVPN, tertiary)

    vmhost dnsmasq (this host)
      → 127.0.2.1:53    (dnscrypt-proxy → Cloudflare DoH, public domains)
      → 10.0.0.{1,2,3} (CorpVPN ZPA DNS, corporate domains via corpvpn-corporate.conf)

## Why dnscrypt-proxy?

Parallels gateway (10.211.55.1) intercepts and blocks outbound UDP port 53.
dnscrypt-proxy uses HTTPS (port 443) for DNS-over-HTTPS, bypassing the block.
TCP DNS also works: dig @1.1.1.1 +tcp

## Split DNS

Corporate domains resolve to ZPA-internal addresses (10.0.0.x) via CorpVPN.
Public domains resolve via dnscrypt-proxy → Cloudflare DoH.

Without corpvpn-corporate.conf, corporate domains fall through to public DNS
and return public-facing IPs (e.g. corp.example → 184.168.131.241 instead
of 10.0.1.x), breaking ZPA private access.

## CorpVPN tunnel stability — DNS-SD storm (root cause & mitigation)

Symptom: CorpVPNTunnel on the Mac loses its mDNSResponder socket (EPIPE) when
overwhelmed by DNS-SD discovery queries, killing the DNS proxy on 10.0.0.1:53
without killing the tunnel process.

### Two contributing factors (both measured 2026-05-30)

1. TRIGGER (primary, chronic): mDNSResponder fans out DNS-SD browse queries
   across ALL active search domains on every CONFIG RELOAD. Measured: a browse
   alone -> 0 DNS-SD to CorpVPN; a browse + `killall -HUP mDNSResponder` -> ~164.
   The fan-out fires on reload, not on browsing.

2. AMPLIFIER (latent): CorpVPN injects an empty string at
   SupplementalMatchDomains[0]. An empty match domain matches the DNS root, so
   mDNSResponder treats CorpVPN as authoritative for everything and each reload
   fans out across all 23 corporate search domains via the tunnel.

### What did NOT work (do not repeat)

- Removing SupplementalMatchDomains[0] via `scutil set`: CorpVPN re-injects it
  within ~30s. The `State:` key is agent-owned; direct writes do not hold.
- A launchd watcher (com.myorg.corpvpn-dns-fix, StartInterval=30) that re-applied
  the scutil fix every 30s: DISPROVEN and HARMFUL. Each run's `scutil set` forced
  an mDNSResponder config reload -> the very fan-out it tried to prevent, every
  30s. It logged "fix applied" on every cycle (never held). REPEALED -- the plist
  is now com.myorg.corpvpn-dns-fix.plist.disabled; script at
  /usr/local/bin/corpvpn-dns-fix.sh left in place but unused. Do NOT re-enable.

### What DID work -- Mac-side /etc/resolver redirect (Option C)

Redirect the DNS-SD meta-domains to this VM (10.211.55.3), which answers each
instantly with 0.0.0.0 via dns-sd-block.conf -- so the fan-out never traverses
the CorpVPN tunnel. This is static config CorpVPN does NOT manage, so it survives
reconnects (unlike the scutil approach). It works even though the VM is not in
the normal Mac->CorpVPN DNS path, because it intercepts at the source.

  /etc/resolver/_dns-sd._udp                       (bare meta-domain)
  /etc/resolver/_dns-sd._udp.<each corp domain>    (23 files, one per search domain)
  /etc/resolver/_dns-sd._udp.in-addr.arpa          (registers but see limitation)
  /etc/resolver/_dns-sd._udp.ip6.arpa              (registers but see limitation)
  -> each file contains a single line: nameserver 10.211.55.3

Result (measured, reload-triggered): DNS-SD reaching the CorpVPN tunnel dropped
from ~164 to 24 (~85% reduction). After editing, apply with:
  sudo killall -HUP mDNSResponder

LIMITATION -- reverse-zone residual (~24 queries): names like
db._dns-sd._udp.0.55.211.10.in-addr.arpa have _dns-sd._udp as a MID-name label,
so they do NOT end in _dns-sd._udp.in-addr.arpa -- /etc/resolver matches by
trailing suffix only and cannot catch them without keying on the reverse zone
(55.211.10.in-addr.arpa), which would hijack ALL reverse DNS for that subnet
(incl. this VM). These are bounded (4 subnets x 3 subtypes) and do NOT scale with
the search-domain list, so they are accepted residual.

### Regenerating the Option C files (when CorpVPN search domains change)

  SINK=10.211.55.3
  for d in $(scutil --dns | awk '/^resolver #1$/{f=1} f&&/search domain\[/{print $NF} /^resolver #2$/{exit}'); do
    printf 'nameserver %s\n' "$SINK" | sudo tee "/etc/resolver/_dns-sd._udp.$d" >/dev/null
  done
  printf 'nameserver %s\n' "$SINK" | sudo tee /etc/resolver/_dns-sd._udp >/dev/null
  sudo killall -HUP mDNSResponder

### Same longest-suffix trap on the VM side

DNS-SD queries are blocked on this VM in dns-sd-block.conf. The bare wildcard
address=/_dns-sd._udp/ in local.conf does NOT cover *._dns-sd._udp.<corp-domain>
because server=/<corp-domain>/ is a longer suffix and wins (forwards to CorpVPN).
dns-sd-block.conf is LOAD-BEARING -- see the header comments in both files.

### True root-cause fix (not yet done)

Raise with CorpVPN/IT: the agent should not inject an empty SupplementalMatchDomains
entry. That is the only fix that removes the amplifier at source; everything above
is mitigation on a corporate-managed Mac.

## Spread DNS test

Run this to verify the full resolution path is healthy:

  for d in google.com github.com bbc.co.uk \
            corp.example myaccess.corp.example \
            example.com global.example unit2.example; do
    result=$(dig @127.0.0.1 $d +short +time=3 +tries=1 2>&1 | head -1)
    echo "$d → $result"
  done

Expected output (corporate domains should return 10.0.0.x via CorpVPN):
  google.com             → 142.x.x.x   (public)
  github.com             → 140.x.x.x   (public)
  bbc.co.uk              → 151.x.x.x   (public)
  corp.example          → 10.0.1.x  (ZPA internal)
  myaccess.corp.example → 10.0.1.x  (ZPA internal)
  example.com             → 13.x.x.x    (public CDN)
  global.example       → 10.0.1.x  (ZPA internal)
  unit2.example               → 10.0.1.x (ZPA internal)

## Files

  local.conf              — main config: upstreams, caching, DNS-SD wildcard blocks
  corpvpn-corporate.conf  — per-domain forwarders to CorpVPN (10.0.0.{1,2,3})
                            KEEP ENABLED — required for split-DNS ZPA resolution
                            Disabling causes corporate domains to return public IPs
  dns-sd-block.conf       — LOAD-BEARING per-corporate-domain DNS-SD blocks.
                            NOT redundant with the local.conf wildcard (longest-suffix
                            match — see header comments). Do NOT delete/disable.
  docs/README.md          — this file (moved out of the parse path; dnsmasq does not
                            recurse into subdirs, so docs live safely under docs/)

Related config on the Mac (not in this dir):
  /etc/resolver/_dns-sd._udp*  — 26 files redirecting DNS-SD to this VM sink (Option C).
                                 See "CorpVPN tunnel stability" above.
  /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.myorg.corpvpn-dns-fix.plist.disabled
                               — repealed watcher daemon. Left disabled. Do NOT re-enable.

## Notes for future agents

- Do NOT disable corpvpn-corporate.conf without understanding split-DNS impact
- Do NOT delete dns-sd-block.conf as "redundant" — the local.conf wildcard does
  not cover *._dns-sd._udp.<corp-domain> (longest-suffix match wins for server=)
- Do NOT re-enable com.myorg.corpvpn-dns-fix — its 30s scutil churn was the storm
  amplifier, not the fix (see "What did NOT work" above)
- If dnsmasq CPU is high, check for DNS-SD storms: sudo tcpdump -i any udp port 53
  | grep _dns-sd._udp   (top offender historically: db._dns-sd._udp.sub1.corp.internal)
- If dnsmasq hangs (TCP children at max 20), CorpVPN tunnel DNS proxy has died
  → On Mac: sudo launchctl kickstart -k system/com.corpvpn.tunnel
- DNS-SD mitigation lives in two layers: Mac /etc/resolver (Option C, primary) +
  this VM's dns-sd-block.conf (the sink Option C points at)
- /etc/default/dnsmasq has CONFIG_DIR with .disabled exclusion — use that suffix
  to safely disable conf files without removing them
